# Line endings and binary detection.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS (trap T20, found in Phase 1 and caused by that session).
#
# This repository has no `.gitattributes`, and the development checkout runs with
# `core.autocrlf=true` (the Windows default). Without a hint, git guesses whether a file is
# text by looking for NUL bytes -- and the committed demo PDFs contain NO NUL bytes, because
# `make-fixtures.mjs` writes them with **uncompressed** content streams so the text layer stays
# inspectable. Git therefore classified a PDF as text and rewrote every LF as CRLF on the way
# into the object store. The committed `demo-brief.pdf` blob came back at 25,678 bytes against a
# 12,661-byte working file, with every newline doubled. That breaks the `/Length` entries and the
# `xref` offsets, so the file is unparseable -- and it would have surfaced only when a fresh
# clone first read the fixture, in Phase 2 (ingestion) or Phase 4, far from the cause.
#
# `.gitattributes` is also the only place this can be fixed reliably: a note in a README is not
# read by git, and `core.autocrlf` is a per-machine setting that a clone does not inherit.
#
# Verification that this holds: clone the repository to a temporary directory and compare
# `pdfjs-dist`'s extraction of `docs/fixtures/demo-brief.pdf` against the working copy.

# Formats that must round-trip byte for byte. `binary` is `-text -diff`: never converted, never
# diffed as text. Add a line here BEFORE committing a new binary format, not after.
#
# Deliberately NO `* text=auto eol=lf` line yet. Text files in this repository were committed
# under `core.autocrlf=true`, so adding a repo-wide normalisation rule would renormalise every
# document in one commit and bury the real change in noise. Line endings for text are left as
# they are; only the formats where a conversion is *corruption* are pinned here.
*.pdf   binary
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.webp  binary
*.ico   binary
*.bmp   binary
*.mp4   binary
*.mp3   binary
*.wav   binary
*.ogg   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.otf   binary
*.zip   binary
*.gz    binary
*.7z    binary
*.xlsx  binary
*.docx  binary
*.pptx  binary
*.sqlite binary
*.db    binary

# The lockfile is generated, so keep it out of language statistics. NOT `-diff`: a diff
# suppressed is a diff nobody reviews, and `git diff --cached` is how this repository checks
# for a leaked secret before every commit (C7). A 150 KB generated file is not a reason to
# blind the one check that matters most.
pnpm-lock.yaml linguist-generated
